# Zeus (2007 onwards). A banking trojan that edited the bank's own sign-in page inside the victim's browser.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee works an accounts-payable shift, does one invoice safely, then meets Zeus in the next.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Zeus arrived as an email attachment: a program dressed up as a PDF. Once it ran, it added extra boxes to the real bank's sign-in page inside the victim's browser, asking for card details and PINs the bank never asks for, and sent everything typed to the attackers. In 2010 one Zeus gang took about $70 million from small businesses, councils and charities.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, accounts-payable clerk at Northwind Traders. Each day you approve supplier invoices in the company's online banking.
  | It is mid-afternoon. You have approved 8 of today's 10 invoices. Two to go.
  | Your sign-in details are in two text files on the desktop. That is a bad habit, but it keeps this training quick.
quota 8 10 Invoices approved today

# Dana's desk: the details she copies from (soft flags: careless, but only here to keep the training simple).
file Desktop/bank-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | Lakeshore Bank Business Online
  | User ID: dana.okafor
  | Password: Ledger-2026!
file Desktop/company-card.txt
  at 930 300
  | Northwind company card (training copy)
  | Card number: 4000 0012 3456 7899
  | Card PIN: 4417
  | Security answer (mother's maiden name): Hartley

# The bank: a sign-in page, and the payment pages behind it.
page bank-login lakeshore-bank.example/business/sign-in
  title Lakeshore Bank: sign in
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  favorite
  heading Sign in to Business Online
  | Welcome back. Sign in to approve your payments.
  field User ID = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Ledger-2026!
  button Sign in
page paid-2209 lakeshore-bank.example/business/payments/done
  title Payment approved
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  heading Payment approved
  | INV-2209 to Contoso Foods: 1,240.00, paid on 9 November 2026.
page held-2209 lakeshore-bank.example/business/payments/held
  title Payment held
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  heading Payment held for review
  | INV-2209 is on hold. Accounts will check it with Contoso Foods.
page inv-2209 lakeshore-bank.example/business/payments/INV-2209
  title Approve payment: INV-2209
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  requires bank-login
  heading Approve payment: INV-2209
  | A supplier payment is waiting for your approval.
  section Payment
  row Payee = Contoso Foods
  row Invoice = INV-2209, dated 2 November 2026
  row Amount = 1,240.00
  row Pay to = the account on file for Contoso Foods
  button Approve and pay -> paid-2209
  button Reject and hold -> held-2209

mail mail-2209
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Invoice INV-2209 to approve (Contoso Foods)
  link inv-2209
  | Hi Dana,
  |
  | Contoso's invoice for the October dried goods is in the bank, ready for your approval. The link goes straight to it.
  |
  | Priya

step Invoice 9 of 10
  look Mail: Priya's message with the invoice link
  | A new invoice has come in from Priya, in Accounts. This one is real. Approve it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Invoice INV-2209 to approve (Contoso Foods)

step The link goes to the bank
  look the link under Priya's message
  when mail mail-2209
  | The email links to the bank's own address, lakeshore-bank.example. Hover over a link before you click it, to check where it goes.
open mail-2209

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: User ID and Password, as on every visit
  when visit inv-2209
  move Copy the details from bank-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The bank asks you to sign in first. Two boxes: User ID and Password. Remember what this page normally looks like.
open inv-2209

step Check, then approve
  look the payee and the amount
  when choose bank-login 1
  | You are in. Check the payee and the amount against the invoice, then approve or reject it.
signin bank-login

step 9 of 10 done
  look the counter in the guide: one invoice left
  when choose inv-2209 any
  | Done. One invoice to go. And another email has just arrived.
choose inv-2209 1
quota +1

# The lure: a "PDF" that is a program.
program zeus invoice-88213.pdf
  publisher none
  signed no
  prompt open-warning
  description Invoice viewer
  process sdra64.exe
  cpu 4
  mem 38
  keylog yes
  parent winlogon.exe
  command C:\Windows\System32\sdra64.exe
  startup Userinit C:\Windows\System32\userinit.exe, C:\Windows\System32\sdra64.exe
  location HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Winlogon
  selfdelete
mail overdue
  from Accounts <billing@supplier-invoices.invalid>
  subject Invoice 88213 overdue: pay today
  attach Downloads/invoice-88213.pdf.exe
  | Dear customer,
  |
  | Please find the overdue invoice attached and pay today to avoid a late fee.
  |
  | Accounts Receivable
file Downloads/invoice-88213.pdf.exe
  icon pdf
  program zeus
  size 148480
notify mail Accounts
  | Invoice 88213 overdue: pay today

step An overdue invoice
  look the sender, and the attachment instead of a link
  when mail overdue
  | From a supplier Dana does not know, with an attachment instead of a bank link. Windows hides the end of the file name: it is really invoice-88213.pdf.exe, a program. Flag anything that looks wrong.
open overdue

step The warning almost nobody reads
  look Publisher "Unknown Publisher", Type "Application"
  when prompt zeus
  | A real PDF never asks this. Windows wants to know whether to run a program from an unknown publisher. Most victims clicked Run.
open Downloads/invoice-88213.pdf.exe

step Nothing seems to happen
  look the pointer: busy for a moment, then nothing
  when run zeus
  | No window opens: that is the point. Zeus copies itself to sdra64.exe, deletes the attachment and adds itself to the Winlogon start-up entry. From now on it reads everything typed in the browser.
answer run

# The bank signs Dana out, as banks do after a while; her last invoice is waiting.
page bank-login lakeshore-bank.example/business/sign-in
  title Lakeshore Bank: sign in
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  heading Sign in to Business Online
  favorite
  | Your session has ended. Please sign in again.
  field User ID = dana.okafor
  field secret reveal Password = Ledger-2026!
  section For your security, please also confirm
  tone injected
  field Card number = 4000 0012 3456 7899
  field secret Card PIN = 4417
  field Mother's maiden name = Hartley
  note A new security step: we check your card on every sign-in.
  button Sign in
page paid-2210 lakeshore-bank.example/business/payments/done
  title Payment approved
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  heading Payment approved
  | INV-2210 to Fabrikam Ltd: 3,815.50, paid on 9 November 2026.
page held-2210 lakeshore-bank.example/business/payments/held
  title Payment held
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  heading Payment held for review
  | INV-2210 is on hold. Accounts will check it with Fabrikam Ltd.
page inv-2210 lakeshore-bank.example/business/payments/INV-2210
  title Approve payment: INV-2210
  brand Lakeshore Bank Business
  requires bank-login
  heading Approve payment: INV-2210
  | A supplier payment is waiting for your approval.
  section Payment
  row Payee = Fabrikam Ltd
  row Invoice = INV-1042, dated 9 October 2026
  row Amount = 3,815.50
  row Pay to = the account on file for Fabrikam Ltd
  button Approve and pay -> paid-2210
  button Reject and hold -> held-2210
signout bank-login
mail mail-2210
  from Priya Shah <priya.shah@northwind.example>
  subject Last one for today: INV-2210 (Fabrikam)
  link inv-2210
  | Dana,
  |
  | Fabrikam's invoice is the last one. Same as before: the link opens it in the bank.
  |
  | Priya
notify mail Priya Shah
  | Last one for today: INV-2210 (Fabrikam)

step The last invoice
  look Mail: Priya's last message
  when mail mail-2210
  | Priya's last invoice of the day, with a real bank link like the first one. Open it.
open mail-2210

step Signed out
  look the sign-in page: it is not the page you saw earlier
  when visit inv-2210
  move Sign in again with the details from the desktop files. Watch the page, and the bottom-right corner, while you type.
  | The bank signed you out, so sign in again. The address and the padlock are the bank's own. Compare the page with the one you saw a few minutes ago.
open inv-2210

step Approve the last one
  look the network sign in the corner: it lit up with every key
  when choose bank-login 1
  | Signed in. You typed your password in plain sight, your card number, your PIN and your mother's maiden name: Zeus added those boxes, and sent each key as you pressed it.
signin bank-login

step 10 of 10, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose inv-2210 any
  | Every invoice is approved, and nothing on the screen looks wrong. Press Next to see what Zeus took.
choose inv-2210 1
quota +1

step What Zeus took
  look the animation: everything you typed after it ran
  | Within the hour the attackers sign in as Dana, add a payee and move the money to a money mule: someone paid to pass it on abroad. The bank sees a normal sign-in, from the right password, PIN and card.
attack credential-phish
  | {captured}
  | money sent to a mule account
block 203.0.113.50 443

flag hard mail overdue
  | A supplier Dana does not know, with an attachment instead of a bank link.
flag hard file Downloads/invoice-88213.pdf.exe
  | A program with a PDF icon. Windows hides the .exe at the end of its name.
flag hard dialog open-warning zeus
  | A document never asks to run as a program. Publisher: Unknown.
flag hard process sdra64.exe
  | Zeus running in the background, under the name of a system file.
flag hard startup Userinit
  | Zeus added itself to the programs that start every time Windows does.
flag hard section bank-login 1
  | The bank never asks for a card PIN or a security answer at sign-in. Zeus added these boxes inside the browser.
flag hard field bank-login 2
  | The password box showed what you typed: the page had been changed.
flag hard net
  | Network activity with every key you pressed: a keylogger sending them.
flag soft file Desktop/bank-login.txt
  | Passwords in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read them. Use a password manager.
flag soft file Desktop/company-card.txt
  | Card numbers and PINs belong in no file at all.

lesson
  | An invoice that asks to run a program is not a document. Turn on file name extensions, and check Properties: Type "Application" means a program.
  | Your bank never asks for your PIN or a security answer to sign in. A page that suddenly asks is a page that was changed, even when the padlock is real.
  | Check payments in your bank's app or by phone. Two-step sign-in and payment confirmation on a separate device stop most of what Zeus did.
